entioned which creates moments  upon moments upon moments is that  if that's fundamentally what the world  is  we posited that it was not reversible  and then we of course have a lot to  explain because many of the laws on the  intermediate scales like  electromagnetism  are reversible  another thing to come to your question  about multiverses  we found it within the work with marina  very important to  assume that there is only one universe  and that  has consequences  for the ways those systems  evolve and i i it's a little bit too  complicated to explain that here  but i don't know if the postulate of  many universes  is helpful i i had a version of it as  well  but the postulate that there is only one  universe seems to me very fruitful and a  question pops into my mind lee um that  if uh laws have evolved and are emergent  in this way is there anything inevitable  about that could we have ended up uh  evolving different laws just as  evolution is contingent  um that's a very very interesting  question i don't i certainly don't know  enough  and i hear i follow paul  and also recently my colleagues molina  and andrew and stu kaufman  in using biology not just to fit biology  into physics but as paul has been doing  and sarah for some time  at your center to use biology as an  example  of how we're to think  of these kinds of questions  that's that's a really interesting  perspective that biology can be useful  to physics and not just the other way  around  and by thanking the speakers the  panelists  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to the institute of art  and ideas at iai tv

